          "url": "https://gearstrings.com/bass/skreddy-pedals-releases-the-rubber-soul",
          "host": "gearstrings.com",
          "title": "Skreddy Pedals Releases The Rubber Soul: A Deep Dive Into Its Circuit Design, Bass-Specific Voicing, and Rhythm Section Integration | GearStrings",
          "h1": "Skreddy Pedals Releases The Rubber Soul: A Deep Dive Into Its Circuit Design, Bass-Specific Voicing, and Rhythm Section Integration",
          "description": "Skreddy Pedals’ new Rubber Soul overdrive delivers authentic Beatles-era tone with bass-optimized headroom, dual clipping paths, and studio-grade signal integrity — reviewed by a working bassist and rhythm section specialist.",
          "snippet": "Skreddy Pedals Releases The Rubber Soul: A Deep Dive Into Its Circuit ...",
          "bodyExcerpt": "Skreddy Pedals Releases The Rubber Soul: A Deep Dive Into Its Circuit Design, Bass-Specific Voicing, and Rhythm Section Integration | GearStrings GEARSTRINGS Home Blog Categories Authors About Home / Blog / bass bass Skreddy Pedals Releases The Rubber Soul: A Deep Dive Into Its Circuit Design, Bass-Specific Voicing, and Rhythm Section Integration By Marcus Reeve July 12, 2026 Skreddy Pedals has launched the Rubber Soul — a meticulously voiced overdrive pedal engineered not just for guitarists, but explicitly for bass players and rhythm section professionals who demand dynamic responsiveness, low-end preservation, and vintage-inspired harmonic texture. Unlike generic boutique overdrives, the Rubber Soul features a dedicated 20Hz–5kHz frequency window optimized for fundamental bass frequencies, dual silicon/germanium clipping diodes switchable via rear-panel DIP switches, and a true-bypass footswitch with The Genesis of a Bass-Centric Overdrive Skreddy Pedals founder Dave Niedbalski spent 18 months prototyping the Rubber Soul after field-testing early versions with session bassists including Tony Levin (King Crimson, Peter Gabriel), Pino Palladino (D’Angelo, John Mayer), and Nate Watts (Herbie Hancock, Quincy Jones). Their collective feedback centered on three non-negotiable criteria: zero low-end compression below 120Hz, immediate touch sensitivity across playing dynamics from fingerstyle to pick attack, and zero high-frequency fizz or nasal midrange honk that compromises blend in live mixes. Early iterations used standard JFET-based gain stages, but testing revealed unacceptable intermodulation distortion when layered with kick drum transients — particularly problematic in funk, Motown, and modern indie-rock contexts where bass occupies the critical 60–150Hz zone alongside the snare’s fundamental. Niedbalski pivoted to a discrete Class-A transistor topology inspired by the preamp section of the 1964 Vox AC30 Top Boost channel — specifically its ECC83-driven gain stage and passive tone stack. However, rather than replicate the AC30’s guitar-centric 100Hz–5kHz response, Skreddy extended the input coupling capacitor to 1µF (vs. the typical 0.022µF), lowering the -3dB point to 20Hz. This allows full transmission of sub-harmonic content from extended-range basses like the Fender Precision Bass Plus (with active EMG PJ set) and the Dingwall Prima Artist (tuned B–E–A–D–G–C). Why Standard Guitar Overdrives Fail Bass Players Most overdrives — even highly regarded units like the Ibanez Tube Screamer TS9, Wampler Tumnus, or Fulltone OCD — are designed with guitar’s 82Hz–1.2kHz fundamental range in mind. Their tone stacks attenuate frequencies below 100Hz by 12–18dB, and their clipping diodes saturate asymmetrically above 200mV, causing premature compression in bass signals averaging 1.2–1.8V RMS. In blind A/B tests conducted at EastWest Studios (Los Angeles) with engineer Chris Lord-Alge, bassists reported diminished note definition, loss of string articulation on muted eighth-note grooves, and phase cancellation issues when tracking through Neve 1073 preamps. The Rubber Soul solves this by incorporating a custom 10kΩ low-Z buffer stage post-clipping — reducing output impedance to 470Ω (vs. 10kΩ typical) — ensuring stable interaction with both passive and active bass pickups and preventing high-frequency roll-off into long cable runs. Circuit Architecture: Dual Clipping, Dynamic Headroom, and Signal Integrity The Rubber Soul’s core innovation lies in its dual-path clipping architecture. Rather than relying on a single pair of diodes, it routes signal through two independent clipping cells: one using soft-saturation silicon diodes (1N914), the other employing asymmetrical germanium diodes (OA90) biased at 1.8V. A rear-mounted 3-position DIP switch selects between Silicon Only, Germanium Only, or Blend Mode — where germanium handles low-mid saturation (120–400Hz) while silicon clips upper harmonics (800Hz–3.2kHz). This preserves transient ",
